   <p><strong>Data.</strong> Pool refreshed 2026-10-14 under the roster validation lock — 335 rows, every placement cross-checked against ESPN’s live rosters (direct-complete, all 30 teams, zero mismatches, 335/335 on the feed, no exemption). The lock (10/14 afternoon, the owner’s call): the six-way blend was taken again from our own lines with Yahoo’s and Hashtag’s 10/14 projections in place of their 10/13 and 10/12 versions — RotoBaller, FantasyPros and the Basketball Monster cards unchanged, same method, games, minutes and placements untouched. 238 of the 335 rows are blended (Obi Toppin newly, now that three outlets project him; Luke Kornet keeps his restored line); 54 lines move, Rui Hachimura (+1.3 points), De’Andre Hunter (−1.0) and Jonathan Kuminga (+0.9) the most. By the owner’s call Kawhi Leonard and Anthony Davis go back to the full injury-risk tier (×0.78 on value); Kyrie Irving and Damian Lillard stay on the lighter one. This refresh (10/14, the final morning pull before the draft) carries the owner’s three lock calls on top of the night’s work. Dylan Cardwell is out at least two weeks with a left calf strain (Anthony Slater of ESPN.com on ESPN’s injuries feed, a 10/29 estimate), and Coby White’s camp calf strain is his third in about a year (out the whole preseason, a 10/21 estimate): both now carry the injury-risk tag (×0.78 on value) and the kit’s games drop by six, 65 to 59 and 68 to 62, the shape used for Stewart and Raynaud; neither moves on the board, because the tag only bites above the pool average. Luke Kornet’s own pre-blend line is restored: every preseason reading backs our 21 minutes for him over the outside average of 17. The 24 players whose projected minutes sit 3 or more from the outside average were read against every preseason box score (46 games, 9/28–10/13) — preseason backs the outside minutes for 9 (Harper, Jalen Green, Demin, Sharpe, Porter, Caruso, Lillard, LaMelo Ball, Dunn), ours for 1 (Kornet), 6 split and 8 have too few games; ESPN’s injuries feed items dated 10/14 are on the players’ notes. No other stat line, tag, team or placement moved. The night of 10/13 did two things. First, the stat lines: at the owner’s request every line with three or more outside projections — 238 of the 335 rows — is now the equal-weight average of our line and the outside lines (Yahoo 10/13, Hashtag 10/12, RotoBaller 10/13, FantasyPros 10/13, Basketball Monster cards 10/13), each of the nine categories separately, ours counting one of up to six; shot attempts were rescaled so points still add up. Before the blend our lines sat as close to the other five as any of them sat to the rest; the average of several such forecasts has a smaller expected error than any one of them. Games, minutes, injury tags and placements did not move; rows with fewer than three outside projections keep our line. Second, availability tags only: Tyrese Haliburton and Walker Kessler are untagged, healthy by the owner’s call: Haliburton started at Oklahoma City and scored 24 in 18 minutes in his first game since Monday’s ankle tweak, Kessler started against Golden State (ESPN box scores). Kawhi Leonard, Anthony Davis, Kyrie Irving and Damian Lillard moved to a new lighter risk tier the owner asked for (Kawhi and Davis back at the lock, above), ×0.85 on value instead of ×0.78 (and 0.79 instead of 0.75 in the weekly model): the outside projections put their games at 0.84–0.87 of a healthy season (Yahoo 10/13, Hashtag 10/12, FantasyPros 10/13). Kawhi debuted tonight, starting and scoring 21 in 17 minutes against the Knicks; Lillard started at Sacramento. Chet Holmgren stays untagged by the owner’s call after missing a second straight game (a quadriceps bruise, day-to-day, a 10/15 estimate on ESPN’s feed). Anthony Black (ankle), Sam Merrill (soreness) and Isaiah Hartenstein (back tonight, 13 minutes) keep their defaults. Yaxel Lendeborg started for Golden State, 26 minutes; the owner plans to take him in the seventh or eighth round. The 10/13 evening range clamp of 24 lines is folded into the blend: those lines were its starting point. Market: Yahoo’s 2026-10-14 paste (the lock paste) prices 302 of 335 rows (ADP on 185, else XRank); the rest ride the internal model.</p>
-  <p><strong>Logic.</strong> Card rows (owner, 2026-10-08) show the market rank, the plus-categories and the 3rd-team warning only; a player’s research note sits behind the muted † at the end of that line (tap or hover), and a risk row’s note behind its ▲. The board, punt math, feed parsing, and council ruleset are ports of <code>scripts/hoops.py</code> / <code>arena/arena.py</code>, verified against engine output at build time; z-scores standardize over the draftable top-156 (zero at that pool’s mean — about the 58th playable row — codified 2026-07-30; the ×0.78 availability multiplier applies above that zero only, audit 2026-10-09). Every layer beyond the composite ranking is <em>display-only and causally tested</em>: Strengths/Weaknesses are your live category ranks vs the field (🔒 = dominated); the ⏱ BUY NOW / TOSS-UP markers and the 🚌 wait-chain are back (<em>refit 2026-09-22</em>): the room-mix survival model behind them was fit on value-drafting mock seats and, across the first two live human rooms from the real seat, read BUY NOW on 93 of 98 scored rows while 65 of those players survived to the next turn (Brier 0.651; suspended 2026-09-21 in between) — the refit prices survival off Yahoo's own price alone (ADP, else XRank; Φ((price − N)/max(8, 0.30·price)); Brier 0.192 on the same rows, quiet rows survived 40 of 43, BUY NOW gone 10 of 15, TOSS-UP a coin flip), so read BUY NOW as two-in-three, not a certainty; the single 🎯 marks the system pick — the top blend50 score (50% marginal weekly-cats-won vs the room + 50% balanced value, deliberately punt-blind; per-player punt numbers ride the blue chips), or an urgent structural TARGET when a needed family's shelf is nearly empty — since 2026-09-21 only if that player carries no availability multiplier and sits within 0.05 cats/week of the #1's ΔECW (mock 51 retro: the override had pinned a 0.78-availability center 0.14 cats/week behind a hindsight-best #1); exact blend ties order by ΔECW before name (same retro: 2 of 13 turns on the deck drafted against were decided alphabetically); the advice line under the BUILD strip states the margin to the #2 in categories per week when the 🎯 leads on ΔECW, and names value as the reason when he trails on it (owner-reported 2026-09-30: a minus-signed margin read backwards); a TARGET label always reflects a roster need, while the same read renders as BOARD LEAN when it only describes what the board is offering (provenance split 2026-08-03, owner-reported); the same read also pins a sixth row under the Top-5 when its family made none of them — <em>LAST CALL</em> when urgent (the 🎯 moves onto it: take it over the Top-5) or <em>DEPTH WATCH</em> when informational (the 🎯 above stays the pick), named 2026-08-10 on owner request. The build-coherence strip checks your declared targets against every single-swap alternative on the roster as drafted (ON TARGET / OFF TARGET / WRONG TARGETS; added 2026-08-05 after mock 32 punted steals over a rank-3-steals roster) — since 2026-09-21 it and the punt advisor read the <em>room</em>: expected weekly category wins from the same weekly model the card scores with, not 13-man z-sum ranks (mock 51 retro: the old read proposed punting turnovers while the roster was beating 76% of the room in them), a lean must hold on two consecutive owner turns before the advisor says the word, and both are <em>advice only</em> — no button writes the punt box (the FULL TILT / Adopt / Retarget buttons sit behind <code>PUNT_BUTTONS</code>, off). The style/archetype layer runs internally (TARGET) and never prescribes the punt box, because punt-<em>declaring</em> measured −5 to −11pp on the original instrument (−3.1 to −6.4pp re-measured on the corrected bench weight, 2026-08-10 — direction survives at every calibration). Mock opponents are the 11 named league-mates (three-season behavioral profiles, E18), seated since 2026-10-06 in the league's real 2026-27 draft order whenever you start a MOCK from slot 10 (owner-supplied, final for the 10/14 draft: Oblena, Noah, Will, Robby, Kyle, Martin, John, JCo, Kevin, you at 10, Cayas, Hegi; any other slot or size falls back to the per-draft shuffle); the synthetic market carries only ~0.45× the real board's divergence, so expect REAL rooms to produce deeper star falls than mocks until a Yahoo price paste replaces the proxy (F8, 2026-09-22: the build bakes Yahoo ADP, else XRank, into the Mkt rank whenever the kit carries a paste; the header tooltip says which this build is). The judgment layer (per-player adjustments, ~marked estimates) is re-authored with each republish — enforced by build gate 5, which refuses any build whose judgment date trails the pull — and never applied to the mock AIs. An owner veto list (<code>JUDGMENT.doNotDraft</code>; 2026-09-28: Porziņģis, the owner's call) removes names from YOUR candidate pool only — the card, the 🎯 and the LAST CALL row never show them — while the room, the resolver, rosters, the matrix, category ranks and the mock AIs keep seeing them; the Best-available table marks them ⛔ DO NOT DRAFT and a my: pick of one logs a warning, not a refusal. Three live-room lessons from mock 54 (2026-09-28): a my: pick that is not the 🎯 shows its card rank and gap under the feed as you type and on the log line — from round 9 on the card beat such picks 15 of 16 times across four rooms, so the owner's rule is to take the 🎯 there; the pick typed right after an UNKNOWN fixes that UNKNOWN in place when it matches the unknown text, otherwise it logs and the status strip keeps naming every open UNKNOWN until it is fixed; and when the advisor calls a category dead, the read names the reach it is warning against — the best remaining player in that category against the 🎯. Two lessons from mock 59 (2026-10-01): after an Insert-at-# the history box now re-numbers and re-seats every earlier line from the state, marks the picks the shift moved onto your seat and gives them the card echo against the card as it stands now, and a standing UNKNOWN takes its new number everywhere; and the card now also reads the next <em>two</em> turns — when the #1 is a man the room prices far below his value (at least 0.60 to survive to your next pick) and a near-equal row would be lost, the advice line says who to take now and who likely waits, with the pair's gain in categories per week (<code>pairDecision</code>, guards 0.01 cats/week and 0.60 survival) — as <em>advice only</em>: a pre-registered replay of the eight public live rooms put the two-pick marker at 45.69 / 45.89 mean title odds against 45.83 / 46.21 for the blend 🎯 on two seed sets (one room better, one worse, five identical), so the marker stays on the blend #1 (<code>PAIR_MARKER</code> off) until a live room says otherwise. Two decisions from the first three MOCK rooms on the real seating (2026-10-07, owner-reported: the late card had offered Cam Thomas and Saddiq Bey over Castle, Mitchell, Barrett, Hachimura and Wagner): an unsigned free agent after camp opened is tagged <em>out-unsigned</em> and leaves every board — Thomas, Ivey, Ball, Dillingham — until two independent outlets report a signing (D-CAST-1; the room had drafted Thomas at #154 on his 2024-25 Brooklyn line); and from round 11 the card's candidates — the council shortlist, the blend50 order and the sixth-row pin — are the rows Yahoo prices (ADP, else XRank), because in the late rounds a man Yahoo does not list is a waiver claim, not a pick (D-CAST-4, <code>CARD_PRICED_FROM</code>; the whole pool returns when fewer than five priced rows remain; ranks, the matrix, the shelf count, the trap line and the advisor keep the whole pool; on the three rooms the rule removed Goodwin, Ellis and Thomas from the round-13 card and left rounds 11–12 within one row). Bey stays: Yahoo and Hashtag both price him, and on last season's line he out-produced the five named guards in 9-cat (rank 85 against 164–228); the harsher committed lines on Castle and Barrett were re-derived the same evening as the first WO-5 step (D-CAST-2, see Data). The mock bots changed once, by experiment (D-CAST-3, pre-registered, N=30 against the ten human rooms on file): from round 7 their value axis is Yahoo's own XRank where the build baked one (<code>BOT_XRANK_FROM</code>; rounds 1–6 unchanged, so the eleven personalities are untouched where the fidelity guard measures them) — mean pick gap to the humans 9.29 against 12.07, consensus names left undrafted 7.2 against 10.43 per room, and the cast now drafts Castle, Barrett and Brooks; the variant that used XRank in every round had won both metrics and erased the personalities, and did not ship. Full evidence trail: <code>arena/results/</code>. Your roster lists every pick: a player no open slot can hold (say, one C among 13 with the bench full) shows in an EXTRA row, and the open slot stays shown as open (2026-10-12). Draft state autosaves in this browser; export <code>draft_state.json</code> for post-draft analysis.</p>
